{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y2fnppGXmS7WWj8PRXUYXm","type":"tool_result","content":"docs/paper/analysis.tex:23:CodeLlama-7B reinforces this: the highest concentration tested ($0.80$) with an extreme spike ($t_1/t_5{=}0.81$, L1H1 contributes $81\\%$ of top-5 delta, $11.3\\times$ the second-ranked head) confirms that even very high concentration yields no benefit when dominated by a single outlier. %exp_id=codellama_hires_profiling\ndocs/paper/analysis.tex:53:CodeLlama-7B & 1:1 & 0.80 & 0.81 & --- & --- & Spike \\\\\ndocs/paper/analysis.tex:69:\\item \\textbf{Spike} ($t_1/t_5 > 0.70$ \\emph{and} concentration $\\geq 0.25$): use uniform. A dominant outlier with no cross-bitwidth gradient cannot be exploited. Yi-1.5 (0.71, 0.90), CodeLlama (0.80, 0.81), and Llama (0.57, 0.85): no benefit.\ndocs/paper/analysis.tex:73:Both conditions are needed: CodeLlama ($0.80$), Llama ($0.57$), and Yi-1.5 ($0.71$) all exceed the concentration threshold yet show no MCKP benefit; their spike shapes ($t_1/t_5 \\geq 0.70$) correctly override.\ndocs/paper/analysis.tex:75:The rule correctly classifies all nine evaluated models (9/9), including the within-family scale contrast (Qwen-7B spread vs.\\ 14B flat) and two MHA architectures (CodeLlama-7B spike, Pythia-1.4B flat).\ndocs/paper/conclusion.tex:5:Whether non-uniform allocation helps depends on the \\emph{shape} of head sensitivity heterogeneity---spread patterns (Qwen-7B, $t_1/t_5{=}0.57$) yield large gains, while spike (Yi-1.5, CodeLlama, Llama) and flat (Mistral, Gemma-2, Phi-3-medium, Qwen-14B, Pythia) patterns show no benefit.\ndocs/paper/introduction.tex:32:The difference traces to the \\emph{distribution shape} of head sensitivity, not GQA ratio or model scale alone: Qwen-7B's Layer-0 heads form a cluster of high sensitivity across multiple bitwidths (``spread''); Yi-1.5, CodeLlama, and Llama have dominant outliers (``spike''); Gemma-2, Mistral, Phi-3-medium, Qwen-14B, and Pythia show near-uniform or diluted sensitivity (``flat'')."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01MG8RGMZBgpjvNJWxBwBHSt","session_id":"f6b0b3c4-17a1-4fb2-92fd-0f4f71a4e809","uuid":"2cd49b24-d371-4176-8688-c72f9ec29195","timestamp":"2026-05-16T02:55:31.657Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a69bd62d2c8fcf417","tool_use_id":"toolu_01BNN8RCpfs7XF3V9DJiYxzo","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R2: Novelty + Related Work Review","usage":{"total_tokens":41665,"tool_uses":11,"duration_ms":115093},"uuid":"3a6568ae-5d2c-496b-b08a-b12012cdcad2","session_id":"f6b0b3c4-17a1-4fb2-92fd-0f4f71a4e809"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BNN8RCpfs7XF3V9DJiYxzo","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the paper. Let me produce the review.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThe paper empirically characterizes 2-bit distortion compounding in per-head KV cache quantization on hardware-aligned bit-widths {2,4,8,16}, proposes a cardinality constraint as a fix, and develops a 2-metric diagnostic for when non-uniform allocation helps. Across nine models, only Qwen2.5-7B benefits meaningfully, making this predominantly a negative result paper with one positive case.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Solver equivalence is a genuinely illuminating experiment.** Showing that constrained greedy and MCKP produce identical allocations (verified at per-head level across 3 architectures, confirmed by brute-force oracle with max gap 0.02 PPL) is a strong empirical contribution that crystallizes the insight: \"the constraint, not the solver, is what matters.\"\n\n2. **Honest scoping and appropriate caveats.** The paper explicitly states N=1 for the spread pattern, calls thresholds \"preliminary,\" notes Qwen-14B dilution as a hypothesis, and avoids overclaiming. The limitations section is thorough. This is refreshingly candid for a venue submission.\n\n3. **Systematic cross-architecture evaluation.** Nine models across seven architecture families (MHA through GQA 8:1), including within-family scale contrast (Qwen-7B vs. 14B), is a rigorous experimental setup. The Yi-1.5 counterexample (high concentration but spike pattern) is particularly well-chosen.\n\n4. **KIVI comparison is fair and informative.** The paper explicitly notes that KIVI subsumes the head-level reallocation signal and that their approach provides independent value only in symmetric-only environments. This is honest positioning that builds trust.\n\n5. **Related work section is comprehensive and well-structured.** The paper cites 31 references spanning KV cache quantization, eviction, weight mixed-precision, and attention head analysis. Differentiation from RateQuant is detailed and technically precise (polymatroid breakdown on non-consecutive bitwidths).\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **(Major) Novelty of the compounding observation is overstated.** RateQuant Appendix M already acknowledges compounding on non-consecutive bitwidths. The paper's claim of \"first quantitative mapping\" is technically defensible (RateQuant acknowledged but did not characterize), but the core observation---that 2-bit errors compound super-additively---is not surprising given the residual stream architecture and the known error accumulation literature in weight quantization. The U-shaped curve is a direct consequence of the trivial tradeoff: more 2-bit heads = more budget for high-bit heads on sensitive positions vs. compounding penalty. The paper systematizes this rather than discovers it.\n\n2. **(Major) The cardinality constraint is a hyperparameter cap, not a principled contribution.** Adding a constraint sum(1[b_h=2]) <= k and sweeping k on calibration data is standard hyperparameter tuning. The paper attempts to elevate this to a \"method contribution\" but it is essentially: \"don't put too many heads at 2-bit.\" Any practitioner who observed degradation would try this. The paper's value lies in showing *why* this is necessary and *when* it helps, not in the constraint itself.\n\n3. **(Major) Only 1/9 models benefit, raising the question of practical relevance.** The paper's strongest result (29.4% PPL reduction on Qwen-7B) applies to exactly one model. The diagnostic classifying 9/9 models is a correct classification of a nearly trivial split (8 negatives, 1 positive). A constant predictor returning \"no benefit\" achieves 8/9. The practical message is: \"don't bother with per-head mixed-precision on hardware-aligned bitwidths unless your model is Qwen-7B-like.\" While this negative result has informational value, it significantly diminishes the method's impact.\n\n4. **(Major) Missing comparison with QAQ.** The introduction mentions QAQ (quality-adaptive quantization) as prior work, but it does not appear in the bibliography or experiments. QAQ performs attention-aware mixed-precision at a token level; a comparison would clarify the granularity tradeoff. Similarly, the paper mentions FastGen but only in the eviction context; FastGen also does attention-aware mixed-precision (importance-only heads get low precision).\n\n5. **(Minor) The diagnostic is not independently validated.** The 2-metric rule is fit on all 9 models with no held-out set. With N=1 positive example, the thresholds (concentration >= 0.50, t1/t5 <= 0.70) are essentially drawn around Qwen-7B. The paper acknowledges this but still claims \"9/9 correct\" as if it were a predictive result rather than a descriptive one.\n\n6. **(Minor) Lack of theoretical grounding for compounding.** The paper characterizes compounding empirically (U-shaped curve, super-additive factors) but provides no analysis of *why* the residual stream amplifies 2-bit errors specifically. A simple analytical model (e.g., error propagation through L layers with M 2-bit heads) would strengthen the contribution from \"we observed this\" to \"we understand this.\"\n\n7. **(Minor) KIVI composability result undermines the paper.** On Qwen, KIVI+MCKP = KIVI alone (9.90 PPL). This means that in any deployment where asymmetric per-channel quantization is available (the common case for modern inference engines), the per-head allocation adds nothing. The paper's value is restricted to \"symmetric-only deployment environments,\" which the paper itself notes is a niche.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. RateQuant Appendix M acknowledges compounding on non-consecutive bitwidths. What specifically does your characterization add beyond their acknowledgment? Can you articulate what a practitioner would learn from your Tables 1-2 that they could not infer from RateQuant's disclosure?\n\n2. If the cardinality constraint is the fix, why is sweeping k on calibration data any different from standard hyperparameter tuning (e.g., choosing a learning rate)? What makes this a \"method contribution\" rather than a \"tuning recipe\"?\n\n3. Given that only Qwen-7B benefits: if a practitioner has already profiled their model and found it is not \"spread,\" the recommendation is simply \"use uniform 4-bit.\" Is the diagnostic providing actionable information beyond \"run the profiler and check if non-uniform helps on your calibration set\"?\n\n4. The KIVI composability result shows per-head allocation provides zero benefit when per-channel asymmetric quantization is available. What fraction of production deployment environments are realistically \"symmetric-only\"?\n\n5. Have you tested the diagnostic thresholds on any model *after* they were determined? E.g., DeepSeek-V2 (MLA), Mixtral (MoE), or Command-R?\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n\n**Contribution 1 (Compounding characterization):** Moderate novelty. The phenomenon is not new (RateQuant Appendix M, weight quantization literature), but the systematic quantification across architectures with the U-shaped curve, super-additive factors, and architecture-dependent onset points adds empirical value. The \"first quantitative mapping\" claim is technically correct but overstates the conceptual novelty. This is a careful measurement, not a discovery.\n\n**Contribution 2 (Cardinality constraint):** Low novelty. The constraint sum(1[b_h=2]) <= k is the obvious response to observing that too many 2-bit heads cause degradation. The paper's genuine contribution here is the *solver equivalence* result showing the constraint makes solver choice irrelevant---this is non-obvious and empirically validated. But the constraint itself is a hyperparameter.\n\n**Contribution 3 (When non-uniform helps):** Moderate novelty in framing, low novelty in content. The spread/spike/flat taxonomy is useful descriptively but is derived from and validated on the same 9 models. With N=1 positive, it is a case study rather than a generalizable finding. The question \"when does per-head allocation help\" is the right question and is under-explored in the literature, giving the paper positioning value.\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n\n1. **QAQ (Zhang et al., 2024)** is mentioned in the introduction summary but absent from the bibliography. This is a mixed-precision KV cache method that should be discussed.\n\n2. **MiKV / Per-head eviction quantization hybrids** --- works that combine quantization and eviction at head granularity should be discussed to clarify the boundary.\n\n3. **No discussion of calibration sensitivity in mixed-precision.** The Hessian-based weight quantization literature (HAWQ, HAWQ-V2) extensively discusses how sensitivity estimates can be unreliable; the parallel to per-head profiling unreliability (the core failure mode here) is underexplored.\n\n4. **Missing AQLM/QuIP# family.** While these are weight quantization methods, their treatment of non-additive quantization error (via codebook optimization) is conceptually related to the compounding phenomenon described here.\n\n5. The paper cites KVTuner (Li et al., 2025, ICML) which performs layer-level mixed-precision KV cache quantization. The relationship deserves more discussion: why is per-head better or worse than per-layer for exploiting heterogeneity?\n\n### Framing Issues\n\n1. **Methods paper vs. analysis paper ambiguity.** The paper presents a \"method\" (cardinality constraint) but its main value is empirical analysis (compounding characterization, 1/8 benefit, diagnostic). The contribution list mixes these. The paper would be stronger if it committed to being an analysis/empirical study paper with the constraint as a diagnostic tool rather than a method.\n\n2. **\"Universal fix\" language is misleading.** The constraint prevents compounding, yes, but on 8/9 models it merely matches uniform---it doesn't \"fix\" anything because there was nothing to fix (the flat/spike models don't over-allocate much in the first place). The term \"universal fix\" implies universal benefit; \"universal safety mechanism\" would be more accurate.\n\n3. **The 29.4% improvement headline number applies to one model.** The abstract and introduction lead with this number, creating an expectation that the method broadly helps. A more honest lead would be: \"We find that per-head mixed-precision on hardware-aligned bitwidths rarely helps, and characterize why.\"\n\n4. **Symmetric-only deployment niche is not well-motivated.** The paper positions its contribution for \"symmetric-only deployment environments\" but does not quantify how common these are or name specific hardware that lacks per-channel asymmetric support.\n\n### Overall Assessment\nScore: 3/6\nConfidence: 4/5\n\nThe paper is a thorough empirical study with honest scoping and rigorous experiments, but its novelty is limited: the compounding observation is a careful measurement of a known phenomenon, the cardinality constraint is hyperparameter tuning, and the method helps only 1/9 tested models (further undermined by KIVI composability showing zero benefit when asymmetric quantization is available). The solver equivalence result and the \"when does non-uniform help\" framing are the strongest intellectual contributions, but they are primarily negative results. For a top venue, this paper needs either (a) a principled theoretical explanation of compounding that enables prediction without profiling, (b) demonstration that the diagnostic generalizes to held-out architectures at scale, or (c) a broader set of models where the method provides benefit. As it stands, the paper's core message---\"per-head mixed-precision on {2,4,8,16} rarely helps; here's why and here's how to tell\"---is valuable but incremental for a main conference."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a69bd62d2c8fcf417 (use SendMessage with to: 'a69bd62d2c8fcf417' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 44050\ntool_uses: 11\nduration_ms: 115094</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6b0b3c4-17a1-4fb2-92fd-0f4f71a4e809","uuid":"2c9a87e4-b150-474f-b359-f992c367a15c","timestamp":"2026-05-16T02:56:15.651Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus: **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"Compounding Failures in Per-Head KV Cache Quantization: An Empirical Analysis of When Non-Uniform Allocation Helps\"\n\nThe paper studies per-head mixed-precision KV cache quantization on hardware-aligned bit-widths {2,4,8,16}. Three contributions claimed:\n1. Compounding effect characterization: first quantitative mapping of 2-bit compounding in per-head KV cache quantization\n2. Cardinality constraint as universal fix: ≤k heads at 2-bit prevents compounding, makes solver choice irrelevant\n3. When does non-uniform help: only 1/8 architectures benefits at 4× compression; 2-metric diagnostic (9/9 correct)\n\n## Full Paper Content\n\n### Abstract\nPer-head mixed-precision KV cache quantization promises better quality-compression tradeoffs than uniform bit-width assignment. On hardware-aligned {2,4,8,16}, 2-bit distortions compound non-additively through the residual stream, causing all tested unconstrained solvers to over-allocate 2-bit heads and underperform uniform 4-bit across 8 diverse architectures (degradation 1.07×–3.31×). A simple cardinality constraint (≤k heads at 2-bit) prevents compounding across all tested architectures. Constrained greedy and MCKP produce identical bit allocations (Δ=0.0000). Under 4× compression, only 1/8 architectures benefits from non-uniform allocation. A 2-metric diagnostic (9/9 correct) gives practitioners a simple check. At 8× compression, constraint reduces degradation from 45.9× to 5.9× on Qwen2.5-7B.\n\n### Introduction\n- KV cache is bottleneck for long-context LLM serving\n- Per-head mixed-precision proposed to improve quality-compression tradeoff\n- Hardware supports only {2,4,8,16} — large gap between 2 and 4\n- Discovery: 2-bit distortions compound non-additively; per-head profiling predicts PPL~11 but actual is 32-37 (3× error)\n- All unconstrained solvers over-allocate 2-bit, worse than uniform 4-bit across all 8 architectures\n\nThree contributions:\n1. Compounding effect characterization — \"first quantitative mapping\"\n2. Cardinality constraint — \"universal fix\" (with qualifier \"across all tested architectures\")\n3. When non-uniform helps — 1/8 architectures, diagnostic 9/9 correct\n\nDifferentiation from RateQuant: (a) RateQuant on consecutive {2,...,6} with polymatroid structure; here {2,4,8,16} breaks it; (b) RateQuant acknowledges compounding in Appendix M but doesn't characterize or fix; (c) first systematic study of when non-uniform helps\n\n### Related Work (Section 2)\n- **KV Cache Quantization**: KIVI [2] (uniform per-channel, asymmetric K/V), KVQuant [1] (per-channel sensitivity-aware, non-uniform codebooks), QAQ [3] (attention-aware, quality-adaptive)\n- **Mixed-Precision Allocation**: RateQuant [4] (rate-distortion formulation, continuous closed-form + discrete greedy, provably optimal on consecutive integers; on non-consecutive {2,4,8,16} polymatroid breaks; acknowledges compounding in Appendix M but no characterization/mitigation)\n- **Compounding Effects**: Non-additive error accumulation known in weight quantization [5], but per-head KV cache compounding through residual stream not systematically studied\n\nOnly 5 references cited.\n\n### Method (Section 3)\n- MCKP formulation with cardinality constraint ∑1[b_h=2] ≤ k\n- Per-head sensitivity profiling (distortion delta)\n- Applicability diagnostic: top-5% concentration ≳0.50 AND t1/t5 ≲0.70\n\n### Key Results\n- Table 1: Compounding sweep on Qwen2.5-7B showing superlinear growth\n- Table 2: Cross-architecture — only Qwen benefits (+27.3%), Llama +0.2%, Mistral −0.5%\n- Table 4: Solver equivalence — constrained greedy = constrained MCKP (identical allocations)\n- Table 6: Budget sweep — benefit amplifies with compression (0% at 2×, 29.4% at 4×, 45.9× at 8×)\n- Table 13: Downstream — only Qwen positive (+2.60pp); Yi −0.77pp, Llama −0.99pp, Mistral −0.48pp, Gemma-2 +0.14pp\n- Table 14: Runtime — 8.5% throughput overhead for mixed-precision\n\n### Limitations (stated)\n- 7B-14B scale only\n- Multi-seed CI incomplete\n- 8.5% throughput overhead\n- Diagnostic from n=9\n\n## Review Context (for understanding novelty claims)\n\nKey context from review planning:\n- RateQuant Appendix M already acknowledges compounding → novelty discount\n- Compounding observation valued as empirical contribution but not novel discovery\n- Constraint viewed by some as \"obvious hyperparameter tuning\" rather than principled contribution\n- 1/8 downstream benefit seen as \"this only works on Qwen\"\n- Diagnostic n=9 only observation, not independent claim\n- Paper needs reframing as empirical analysis paper\n- Solver equivalence is the strongest single experiment — proves \"constraint is the contribution, not the solver\"\n- But this raises: \"if a simple cardinality constraint is all you need, is that sufficient contribution for a main conference paper?\"\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on:\n1. **Novelty**: How novel are the contributions relative to prior work, especially RateQuant? Is the compounding characterization truly \"first\"? Is the cardinality constraint a genuine contribution or hyperparameter tuning?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are important baselines and prior work missing? Is the differentiation from RateQuant fair and accurate?\n3. **Contribution significance**: Is the paper's contribution sufficient for a top venue? Is it an analysis paper or a methods paper?\n4. **Framing and positioning**: Is the paper honestly positioned? Are claims appropriately scoped?\n5. **Impact and practical value**: Would practitioners find this useful?\n\nProduce your review in this format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, be specific]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, be specific. Label each as Major or Minor]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n[Detailed analysis of each claimed contribution's novelty]\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n[Missing citations, unfair comparisons, etc.]\n\n### Framing Issues\n[Where does the paper mis-position itself?]\n\n### Overall Assessment\nScore: X/6\nConfidence: X/5\n[2-3 sentence justification]\n```","agentId":"a69bd62d2c8fcf417","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a comprehensive understanding of the paper. Let me produce the review.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\nThe paper empirically characterizes 2-bit distortion compounding in per-head KV cache quantization on hardware-aligned bit-widths {2,4,8,16}, proposes a cardinality constraint as a fix, and develops a 2-metric diagnostic for when non-uniform allocation helps. Across nine models, only Qwen2.5-7B benefits meaningfully, making this predominantly a negative result paper with one positive case.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Solver equivalence is a genuinely illuminating experiment.** Showing that constrained greedy and MCKP produce identical allocations (verified at per-head level across 3 architectures, confirmed by brute-force oracle with max gap 0.02 PPL) is a strong empirical contribution that crystallizes the insight: \"the constraint, not the solver, is what matters.\"\n\n2. **Honest scoping and appropriate caveats.** The paper explicitly states N=1 for the spread pattern, calls thresholds \"preliminary,\" notes Qwen-14B dilution as a hypothesis, and avoids overclaiming. The limitations section is thorough. This is refreshingly candid for a venue submission.\n\n3. **Systematic cross-architecture evaluation.** Nine models across seven architecture families (MHA through GQA 8:1), including within-family scale contrast (Qwen-7B vs. 14B), is a rigorous experimental setup. The Yi-1.5 counterexample (high concentration but spike pattern) is particularly well-chosen.\n\n4. **KIVI comparison is fair and informative.** The paper explicitly notes that KIVI subsumes the head-level reallocation signal and that their approach provides independent value only in symmetric-only environments. This is honest positioning that builds trust.\n\n5. **Related work section is comprehensive and well-structured.** The paper cites 31 references spanning KV cache quantization, eviction, weight mixed-precision, and attention head analysis. Differentiation from RateQuant is detailed and technically precise (polymatroid breakdown on non-consecutive bitwidths).\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **(Major) Novelty of the compounding observation is overstated.** RateQuant Appendix M already acknowledges compounding on non-consecutive bitwidths. The paper's claim of \"first quantitative mapping\" is technically defensible (RateQuant acknowledged but did not characterize), but the core observation---that 2-bit errors compound super-additively---is not surprising given the residual stream architecture and the known error accumulation literature in weight quantization. The U-shaped curve is a direct consequence of the trivial tradeoff: more 2-bit heads = more budget for high-bit heads on sensitive positions vs. compounding penalty. The paper systematizes this rather than discovers it.\n\n2. **(Major) The cardinality constraint is a hyperparameter cap, not a principled contribution.** Adding a constraint sum(1[b_h=2]) <= k and sweeping k on calibration data is standard hyperparameter tuning. The paper attempts to elevate this to a \"method contribution\" but it is essentially: \"don't put too many heads at 2-bit.\" Any practitioner who observed degradation would try this. The paper's value lies in showing *why* this is necessary and *when* it helps, not in the constraint itself.\n\n3. **(Major) Only 1/9 models benefit, raising the question of practical relevance.** The paper's strongest result (29.4% PPL reduction on Qwen-7B) applies to exactly one model. The diagnostic classifying 9/9 models is a correct classification of a nearly trivial split (8 negatives, 1 positive). A constant predictor returning \"no benefit\" achieves 8/9. The practical message is: \"don't bother with per-head mixed-precision on hardware-aligned bitwidths unless your model is Qwen-7B-like.\" While this negative result has informational value, it significantly diminishes the method's impact.\n\n4. **(Major) Missing comparison with QAQ.** The introduction mentions QAQ (quality-adaptive quantization) as prior work, but it does not appear in the bibliography or experiments. QAQ performs attention-aware mixed-precision at a token level; a comparison would clarify the granularity tradeoff. Similarly, the paper mentions FastGen but only in the eviction context; FastGen also does attention-aware mixed-precision (importance-only heads get low precision).\n\n5. **(Minor) The diagnostic is not independently validated.** The 2-metric rule is fit on all 9 models with no held-out set. With N=1 positive example, the thresholds (concentration >= 0.50, t1/t5 <= 0.70) are essentially drawn around Qwen-7B. The paper acknowledges this but still claims \"9/9 correct\" as if it were a predictive result rather than a descriptive one.\n\n6. **(Minor) Lack of theoretical grounding for compounding.** The paper characterizes compounding empirically (U-shaped curve, super-additive factors) but provides no analysis of *why* the residual stream amplifies 2-bit errors specifically. A simple analytical model (e.g., error propagation through L layers with M 2-bit heads) would strengthen the contribution from \"we observed this\" to \"we understand this.\"\n\n7. **(Minor) KIVI composability result undermines the paper.** On Qwen, KIVI+MCKP = KIVI alone (9.90 PPL). This means that in any deployment where asymmetric per-channel quantization is available (the common case for modern inference engines), the per-head allocation adds nothing. The paper's value is restricted to \"symmetric-only deployment environments,\" which the paper itself notes is a niche.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. RateQuant Appendix M acknowledges compounding on non-consecutive bitwidths. What specifically does your characterization add beyond their acknowledgment? Can you articulate what a practitioner would learn from your Tables 1-2 that they could not infer from RateQuant's disclosure?\n\n2. If the cardinality constraint is the fix, why is sweeping k on calibration data any different from standard hyperparameter tuning (e.g., choosing a learning rate)? What makes this a \"method contribution\" rather than a \"tuning recipe\"?\n\n3. Given that only Qwen-7B benefits: if a practitioner has already profiled their model and found it is not \"spread,\" the recommendation is simply \"use uniform 4-bit.\" Is the diagnostic providing actionable information beyond \"run the profiler and check if non-uniform helps on your calibration set\"?\n\n4. The KIVI composability result shows per-head allocation provides zero benefit when per-channel asymmetric quantization is available. What fraction of production deployment environments are realistically \"symmetric-only\"?\n\n5. Have you tested the diagnostic thresholds on any model *after* they were determined? E.g., DeepSeek-V2 (MLA), Mixtral (MoE), or Command-R?\n\n### Novelty Assessment\n\n**Contribution 1 (Compounding characterization):** Moderate novelty. The phenomenon is not new (RateQuant Appendix M, weight quantization literature), but the systematic quantification across architectures with the U-shaped curve, super-additive factors, and architecture-dependent onset points adds empirical value. The \"first quantitative mapping\" claim is technically correct but overstates the conceptual novelty. This is a careful measurement, not a discovery.\n\n**Contribution 2 (Cardinality constraint):** Low novelty. The constraint sum(1[b_h=2]) <= k is the obvious response to observing that too many 2-bit heads cause degradation. The paper's genuine contribution here is the *solver equivalence* result showing the constraint makes solver choice irrelevant---this is non-obvious and empirically validated. But the constraint itself is a hyperparameter.\n\n**Contribution 3 (When non-uniform helps):** Moderate novelty in framing, low novelty in content. The spread/spike/flat taxonomy is useful descriptively but is derived from and validated on the same 9 models. With N=1 positive, it is a case study rather than a generalizable finding. The question \"when does per-head allocation help\" is the right question and is under-explored in the literature, giving the paper positioning value.\n\n### Related Work Gaps\n\n1. **QAQ (Zhang et al., 2024)** is mentioned in the introduction summary but absent from the bibliography. This is a mixed-precision KV cache method that should be discussed.\n\n2. **MiKV / Per-head eviction quantization hybrids** --- works that combine quantization and eviction at head granularity should be discussed to clarify the boundary.\n\n3. **No discussion of calibration sensitivity in mixed-precision.** The Hessian-based weight quantization literature (HAWQ, HAWQ-V2) extensively discusses how sensitivity estimates can be unreliable; the parallel to per-head profiling unreliability (the core failure mode here) is underexplored.\n\n4. **Missing AQLM/QuIP# family.** While these are weight quantization methods, their treatment of non-additive quantization error (via codebook optimization) is conceptually related to the compounding phenomenon described here.\n\n5. The paper cites KVTuner (Li et al., 2025, ICML) which performs layer-level mixed-precision KV cache quantization. The relationship deserves more discussion: why is per-head better or worse than per-layer for exploiting heterogeneity?\n\n### Framing Issues\n\n1. **Methods paper vs. analysis paper ambiguity.** The paper presents a \"method\" (cardinality constraint) but its main value is empirical analysis (compounding characterization, 1/8 benefit, diagnostic). The contribution list mixes these. The paper would be stronger if it committed to being an analysis/empirical study paper with the constraint as a diagnostic tool rather than a method.\n\n2. **\"Universal fix\" language is misleading.** The constraint prevents compounding, yes, but on 8/9 models it merely matches uniform---it doesn't \"fix\" anything because there was nothing to fix (the flat/spike models don't over-allocate much in the first place). The term \"universal fix\" implies universal benefit; \"universal safety mechanism\" would be more accurate.\n\n3. **The 29.4% improvement headline number applies to one model.** The abstract and introduction lead with this number, creating an expectation that the method broadly helps. A more honest lead would be: \"We find that per-head mixed-precision on hardware-aligned bitwidths rarely helps, and characterize why.\"\n\n4. **Symmetric-only deployment niche is not well-motivated.** The paper positions its contribution for \"symmetric-only deployment environments\" but does not quantify how common these are or name specific hardware that lacks per-channel asymmetric support.\n\n### Overall Assessment\nScore: 3/6\nConfidence: 4/5\n\nThe paper is a thorough empirical study with honest scoping and rigorous experiments, but its novelty is limited: the compounding observation is a careful measurement of a known phenomenon, the cardinality constraint is hyperparameter tuning, and the method helps only 1/9 tested models (further undermined by KIVI composability showing zero benefit when asymmetric quantization is available). The solver equivalence result and the \"when does non-uniform help\" framing are the strongest intellectual contributions, but they are primarily negative results. For a top venue, this paper needs either (a) a principled theoretical explanation of compounding that enables prediction without profiling, (b) demonstration that the diagnostic generalizes to held-out architectures at scale, or (c) a broader set of models where the method provides benefit. As it stands, the paper's core message---\"per-head mixed-precision on {2,4,8,16} rarely helps; here's why and here's how to tell\"---is valuable but incremental for a main conference."}],"totalDurationMs":115094,"totalTokens":44050,"totalToolUseCount":11,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1668,"cache_read_input_tokens":39583,"output_tokens":2798,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1668},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2798,"cache_read_input_tokens":39583,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1668,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1668,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":8,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a30881bec0ec9c539","tool_use_id":"toolu_01MG8RGMZBgpjvNJWxBwBHSt","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1: Methods + Experiments Review","usage":{"total_tokens":66566,"tool_uses":28,"duration_ms":183841},"uuid":"056ab60e-0e29-43fe-91eb-fc03eed900b1","session_id":"f6b0b3c4-17a1-4fb2-92fd-0f4f71a4e809"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MG8RGMZBgpjvNJWxBwBHSt","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a complete picture. CodeLlama-7B is claimed as part of the 9/9 diagnostic but has \"---\" for the actual PPL evaluation (only profiled, not fully evaluated with MCKP). This is a notable gap. Let me now write the review.\n\n---\n\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThe paper identifies 2-bit distortion compounding as a failure mode in per-head KV cache quantization with hardware-aligned bit-widths, proposes a cardinality constraint to prevent it, and characterizes when non-uniform allocation is beneficial via a two-metric diagnostic. The primary positive result is on a single model (Qwen2.5-7B); eight other models show no benefit. The paper is commendably honest about this, but the experimental design is heavily Qwen-centric, with most ablations performed on only one architecture.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Clear problem identification with strong evidence.** The compounding phenomenon is convincingly demonstrated through the U-shaped curve (Table 1/Figure in Appendix A) and the cross-architecture compounding sweep (Figure 2, Section 5.2), showing that the additive distortion assumption breaks down specifically at 2-bit.\n\n2. **Rigorous solver equivalence verification.** The claim of greedy=MCKP is verified at the per-head allocation level (112/112, 256/256, 255/256) across three architectures, not merely at the PPL level, and the per-layer brute-force oracle (Appendix, Section on Profiling Resolution) closes the optimality chain with a maximum gap of 0.018 PPL.\n\n3. **Honest framing of the negative result.** The paper clearly states that only 1/9 models benefits (N=1 spread), qualifies all diagnostic thresholds as \"preliminary,\" and acknowledges the KIVI composability limitation (KIVI+MCKP = KIVI alone on Qwen) in Section 5.1. This is unusually frank for the genre.\n\n4. **Multi-seed stability.** The 3-seed calibration stability analysis (mean PPL 10.99 +/- 0.06, 90.2% allocation agreement) and cross-dataset stability (>96% agreement on 3 architectures, Table in Appendix Section G) strengthen reproducibility claims.\n\n5. **Cross-architecture compounding curves (Figure 2/Section 5.2).** Testing compounding on all three sensitivity-pattern archetypes (Qwen/Llama/Mistral) with sweeps up to k=256 provides convincing evidence that the cardinality constraint operates in a universally safe regime.\n\n6. **Wide architectural coverage.** Eight models spanning MHA, GQA 2:1 through 8:1, sliding-window, and softcapped attention provide reasonable diversity for an empirical study.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **[Major] Qwen-centric ablation design.** The following experiments are conducted only on Qwen2.5-7B: budget sweep (Table in Appendix E), profiling resolution comparison (Appendix I), multi-seed stability (Appendix I), KV separation (Appendix J), runtime benchmark (Appendix K), per-layer oracle validation (Appendix I). Since Qwen is the only \"spread\" model and the only one that benefits, these ablations cannot confirm generalizability. The cardinality constraint is claimed universal, yet its optimal k is tuned per-model with no principled selection rule beyond \"small sweep on calibration data.\"\n\n2. **[Major] Diagnostic rule validated on training set only.** The 9/9 classification accuracy is computed on the same models used to derive the thresholds (concentration >= 0.50, t1/t5 <= 0.70). With only 1 positive example and 8 negatives, any threshold between the positive (0.61, 0.57) and the nearest negative (Llama: 0.57, 0.85) would achieve 9/9. The claim of \"correctly classifies all nine models\" overstates what is effectively curve-fitting with zero held-out validation. The authors acknowledge this (N=1, \"preliminary\"), but it is still prominently repeated in the abstract, introduction, and conclusion.\n\n3. **[Major] CodeLlama-7B counted in 9/9 without end-to-end evaluation.** Table 14 shows \"---\" for both uniform and constrained PPL on CodeLlama. The diagnostic classification relies on profiling data alone, without actually verifying that constrained MCKP provides no benefit. This weakens the claimed 9/9 accuracy---it is really 8/8 verified + 1 inferred from profiling shape.\n\n4. **[Major] Downstream evaluation lacks confidence intervals for 4/5 models.** Only Qwen has multi-seed error bars in Table (Section 5.3). The remaining models (Yi, Llama, Mistral, Gemma-2) are single-seed, with deltas as small as +/-0.1pp, well within typical evaluation noise. The claim that non-spread models show \"near-neutral\" change is not statistically distinguishable from zero.\n\n5. **[Minor] Inconsistent quantization scheme comparison with KIVI.** The KIVI comparison (Section 5.1) reveals that per-head symmetric quantization produces uniform 4-bit PPL of 15.45 on Qwen vs. KIVI's 9.91. The paper correctly identifies this as a scheme difference, but the practical implication---that the entire paper's contribution is irrelevant when per-channel asymmetric quantization is available---is understated. The stated use case (\"symmetric-only deployment environments---common in hardware accelerators that lack per-channel asymmetric support\") is asserted without citation or evidence of such hardware constraints in 2025+.\n\n6. **[Minor] Budget sweep at 8x is uninformative.** At 8x compression, both methods produce catastrophic PPL (907 vs. 14759). Claiming \"an order of magnitude better\" at PPL=907 has no practical utility---neither is usable. The intermediate 5.3x result (Appendix F) is more informative but only covers Llama/Mistral, not Qwen.\n\n7. **[Minor] Runtime benchmark methodology.** The 8.5% overhead is measured with PyTorch-level per-head quantize/dequantize, which the authors acknowledge is not production-realistic. Without kernel-level benchmarks, the throughput claim provides limited guidance for practitioners.\n\n8. **[Minor] Long-context evaluation limited to 8K.** The dramatic result (Qwen uniform 11.15x at 8K) is compelling but only covers 2 models and stops at 8K tokens, far below production context lengths (32K-128K). The claim of \"amplification with longer sequences\" is an extrapolation.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. For the cardinality constraint k selection: is there a principled stopping criterion beyond the minimum of the U-shaped curve? Could you characterize the relationship between k and model properties (head count, GQA ratio) to enable k selection without a compounding sweep?\n\n2. The KIVI+MCKP composability experiment shows no additive benefit. Have you tested whether KIVI with per-head mixed-precision (e.g., some heads at 4-bit KIVI, others at 2-bit KIVI) could recover the benefit?\n\n3. For the compounding sweep (Figure 2), are the k heads selected by the same sensitivity ranking? If so, does the compounding onset depend on which heads are selected (top-k most sensitive vs. top-k least sensitive)?\n\n4. The super-additivity coefficients (1.56x at k=8, 4.89x at k=64) are measured on Qwen. What are the corresponding values for Llama and Mistral?\n\n5. Why does the constrained MCKP produce PPL slightly worse than uniform on Gemma-2 (+0.03) and Phi-3-medium (+0.01)? If the solver knows uniform is feasible, it should not produce worse results unless the cardinality constraint forces unnecessary 2-bit assignments.\n\n### Missing Experiments / Evidence Gaps\n\n1. **Multi-seed downstream evaluation for non-Qwen models.** The current single-seed results cannot support claims about near-zero deltas being meaningful.\n\n2. **CodeLlama end-to-end constrained MCKP evaluation.** Required to honestly claim 9/9.\n\n3. **Held-out validation of the diagnostic rule.** At minimum, one additional model not used for threshold derivation should be tested. The Qwen-14B and Pythia additions are not truly independent since Qwen-14B is the same family and Pythia's flat pattern is trivially classified.\n\n4. **Compounding sweep on non-Qwen models.** The cross-architecture compounding (Figure 2) uses all-heads-sorted-by-sensitivity sweeps, but the U-shaped budget optimization curve (Figure 1) showing the optimal k is only demonstrated on Qwen. How do we know k=2 is optimal for Llama/Mistral without their own U-shaped curves?\n\n5. **Oracle comparison on non-Qwen models.** The per-layer brute-force oracle validates the additive distortion assumption, but only on Qwen. If the assumption holds better on flat/spike models (as expected), showing this explicitly would strengthen the argument.\n\n6. **Runtime benchmark on a model where MCKP helps.** Currently measured only on Qwen, but the \"is it worth the overhead?\" question needs the answer to be on the same model where there is a PPL benefit.\n\n### Claim-Evidence Alignment Issues\n\n1. **\"Solver choice irrelevant\" claim.** The claim is empirically verified on 3 architectures under constrained allocation. However, the theoretical argument (Section 3.4) requires \"no ties in the top-k marginal benefit ranking.\" This condition is not verified for all 9 models---only the 3 explicitly checked. On Mistral, 1/256 differs, attributed to a \"zero-distortion tie with no PPL impact,\" but this edge case slightly undercuts the universality framing.\n\n2. **\"9/9 correct diagnostic.\"** As noted, one model (CodeLlama) lacks end-to-end evaluation, and the rule is fit on all data with no holdout. A more accurate claim would be \"8/8 verified, consistent with 9/9 under profiling-based inference.\"\n\n3. **\"29.4% reduction\" headline number.** The paper uses the high-resolution profiling result (10.91) in the main tables and abstract, which is the best-case Qwen number. The standard-resolution result (11.23, still a 27.3% reduction) and the multi-seed mean (10.99) are buried in appendices. The headline number is technically correct (a single seed under hires profiling) but not the most representative.\n\n4. **Cross-dataset stability \"96% agreement.\"** On Llama, 253/256 heads are forced to 4-bit by the k=2 constraint. The paper acknowledges this inflates agreement (Appendix G), but the headline \">96% on all three architectures\" benefits from this inflation. On Qwen (where all heads are variable), the 96.4% agreement is genuinely informative.\n\n5. **\"Non-uniform allocation provides no meaningful benefit\" for 8/9 models.** On Llama, constrained MCKP achieves 8.45 vs. uniform 8.47 (a 0.02 improvement). While this is negligible practically, the direction is positive. The \"no benefit\" framing is defensible but borders on overstating---more precisely, the benefit is within noise without multi-seed validation.\n\n### Overall Assessment\nScore: 4/6\nConfidence: 4/5\n\nThe paper makes a useful empirical contribution by identifying and fixing 2-bit compounding in per-head KV cache quantization. The methodology is sound (MCKP + cardinality constraint is a clean formulation), the compounding characterization is thorough, and the honest framing that only 1/9 models benefits is refreshing. However, the experimental design is too Qwen-centric for a paper claiming cross-architecture generality: most ablations run on only one model, the diagnostic is fit without holdout, CodeLlama's classification is unverified end-to-end, and downstream results lack error bars on 4/5 models. The practical impact is further limited by the KIVI composability result showing the contribution is subsumed by existing per-channel methods. A solid empirical analysis paper that needs broader ablation coverage and statistical rigor to reach the acceptance bar."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a30881bec0ec9c539 (use SendMessage with to: 'a30881bec0ec9c539' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 69074\ntool_uses: 28\nduration_ms: 183843</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6b0b3c4-17a1-4fb2-92fd-0f4f71a4e809","uuid":"5fe1e12c-a08f-49e4-832f-e0914aa75d63","timestamp":"2026-05-16T02:56:52.206Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus: **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"Compounding Failures in Per-Head KV Cache Quantization: An Empirical Analysis of When Non-Uniform Allocation Helps\"\n\nThe paper studies per-head mixed-precision KV cache quantization on hardware-aligned bit-widths {2,4,8,16}. Main findings:\n1. 2-bit quantization distortions compound non-additively through the residual stream, causing unconstrained solvers (greedy, MCKP) to over-allocate 2-bit heads and underperform uniform 4-bit across 8 architectures (degradation 1.07×–3.31×).\n2. A cardinality constraint (≤k heads at 2-bit, k≤8) prevents compounding and makes solver choice irrelevant (greedy=MCKP, Δ=0.0000).\n3. Under 4× compression, only 1/8 architectures (Qwen2.5-7B) benefits from non-uniform allocation. A 2-metric diagnostic (9/9 correct) classifies when profiling is worthwhile.\n\n## Paper Content (Key Sections)\n\n### Method (Section 3)\n- Problem: assign bit-width b_h ∈ {2,4,8,16} to each KV head h to minimize PPL subject to memory budget\n- Per-head sensitivity profiling: measure distortion delta when one head is quantized while others stay at 16-bit. Uses WikiText-2 calibration. Two resolutions: low-res (16×512) and high-res (64×2048).\n- MCKP formulation with cardinality constraint: ∑1[b_h=2] ≤ k\n- k selected via small-scale compounding test; k≤8 yields <2.5% degradation across all tested architectures\n- Applicability diagnostic: top-5% concentration ≳0.50 AND t1/t5 ≲0.70 → \"spread\" pattern → non-uniform recommended\n\n### Experiments (Section 5)\n\n**Table 2: Cross-architecture results (4× compression)**\n- Qwen2.5-7B: BF16=9.81, Uniform=15.45, Constrained MCKP=10.91 (+27.3% vs uniform)\n- Llama-3.1-8B: BF16=8.09, Uniform=8.47, Constrained MCKP=8.45 (+0.2%)\n- Mistral-7B-v0.3: BF16=7.04, Uniform=7.13, Constrained MCKP=7.17 (−0.5%)\n\n**Table 1: Compounding sweep (Qwen2.5-7B)**\nk=0: PPL=9.81 (1.00×), k=8: 10.05 (1.02×), k=16: 10.42 (1.06×), k=32: 11.60 (1.18×), k=64: 23.22 (2.37×), k=112: 98.75 (10.07×)\n\n**Table 3: Mistral k-ablation** — PPL monotonically increases with k; uniform is optimal\n\n**Table 4: Solver equivalence** — Greedy unconstrained=32.45, MCKP unconstrained=36.92 (both FAIL); Greedy+max_2bit=8: 11.23, MCKP+max_2bit=8: 11.23 (identical, both PASS); Uniform=15.45\n\n**Table 5: Oracle comparison** — MCKP optimal in 5/8 layer groups, max gap=0.014 PPL\n\n**Table 6: Budget sweep (Qwen only)** — 2×: ~0% diff; 4×: 29.4%; 8×: 45.9× (MCKP=57.6 vs uniform=2643.77)\n\n**Table 7: Profiling resolution** — Low-res PPL=11.23, High-res PPL=10.91, Uniform=15.45\n\n**Table 8: L0 exclusion ablation** — Solver auto-discovers L0 sensitivity (identical PPL with/without exclusion)\n\n**Table 9: K/V separation** — Worse than joint (+0.31 PPL) due to K-cache compounding\n\n**Table 10: Heuristic baselines** — MCKP beats L0-random by 5.2% (Qwen), 15.8% (Llama), 5.7% (Mistral)\n\n**Table 11: Cross-dataset calibration** — >96% agreement across 3 architectures (Qwen 96.4%, Llama 97.7%, Mistral 96.5%)\n\n**Table 12: Multi-seed stability** — PPL mean=10.99±0.065 (3 seeds), bit distribution identical\n\n**Table 13: Downstream evaluation (5 architectures)** — Only Qwen shows positive gains (+2.60pp avg). Yi: −0.77pp, Llama: −0.99pp, Mistral: −0.48pp, Gemma-2: +0.14pp. ARC-Challenge shows systematic degradation across non-Spread models.\n\n**Table 14: Runtime** — MCKP throughput 21.8 vs uniform 23.6 tok/s (−8.5% overhead)\n\n### Limitations (stated by authors)\n- Only 7B-14B scale (72B attempt killed)\n- Multi-seed CI for PPL but not all downstream tasks\n- 8.5% throughput overhead\n- Diagnostic thresholds from n=9 models\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review context)\n\nThe following experiments were planned. Check coverage:\n1. L0 Exclusion Removal Ablation — covered (Table 8), Qwen-only\n2. Llama-3.1-8B Full Pipeline — covered (Table 2)\n3. Mistral-7B Full Pipeline — covered (Tables 2, 3)\n4. Compounding Sweep — covered (Table 1), Qwen-only in main paper\n5. Budget Sweep — covered (Table 6), Qwen-only\n6. High-Resolution Profiling — covered (Table 7), Qwen-only\n7. C4 Cross-Dataset — covered (Table 11), 3 architectures\n8. RateQuant Greedy Comparison — covered (Table 4)\n9. Oracle Comparison — covered (Table 5), Qwen-only, only 4 layer groups\n10. Cross-Domain Calibration — merged with #7\n11. Head Sensitivity Visualization — paper mentions heatmap in abstract area but unclear if multi-architecture visualization included\n12. K/V Separation — covered (Table 9), Qwen-only\n13. Downstream Task Evaluation — covered (Table 13), 5 architectures but missing some entries for Mistral/Gemma-2\n14. 70B Profiling — killed, acknowledged in limitations\n15. L0-Only Heuristic — covered (Table 10), 3 architectures\n16. Solver Equivalence — covered (Table 4)\n17. Multi-Seed Error Bars — covered (Table 12), Qwen-only PPL, no downstream CI\n18. Runtime Benchmark — covered (Table 14), Qwen-only\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on:\n1. **Methodological soundness**: Is the MCKP + cardinality constraint formulation rigorous? Is the profiling methodology sound? Is the diagnostic well-motivated?\n2. **Experimental thoroughness**: Are experiments sufficient to support claims? What's missing?\n3. **Statistical rigor**: Are claims backed by proper error bars, confidence intervals, multiple seeds?\n4. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce?\n5. **Claim-evidence alignment**: Do the claims match what the evidence actually shows?\n\nProduce your review in this format:\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, be specific with table/section references]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, be specific. Label each as Major or Minor]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing Experiments / Evidence Gaps\n[What experiments from the design are missing or incomplete in the paper?]\n\n### Claim-Evidence Alignment Issues\n[Where do claims overreach the evidence?]\n\n### Overall Assessment\nScore: X/6\nConfidence: X/5\n[2-3 sentence justification]\n```","agentId":"a30881bec0ec9c539","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a complete picture. CodeLlama-7B is claimed as part of the 9/9 diagnostic but has \"---\" for the actual PPL evaluation (only profiled, not fully evaluated with MCKP). This is a notable gap. Let me now write the review.\n\n---\n\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThe paper identifies 2-bit distortion compounding as a failure mode in per-head KV cache quantization with hardware-aligned bit-widths, proposes a cardinality constraint to prevent it, and characterizes when non-uniform allocation is beneficial via a two-metric diagnostic. The primary positive result is on a single model (Qwen2.5-7B); eight other models show no benefit. The paper is commendably honest about this, but the experimental design is heavily Qwen-centric, with most ablations performed on only one architecture.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Clear problem identification with strong evidence.** The compounding phenomenon is convincingly demonstrated through the U-shaped curve (Table 1/Figure in Appendix A) and the cross-architecture compounding sweep (Figure 2, Section 5.2), showing that the additive distortion assumption breaks down specifically at 2-bit.\n\n2. **Rigorous solver equivalence verification.** The claim of greedy=MCKP is verified at the per-head allocation level (112/112, 256/256, 255/256) across three architectures, not merely at the PPL level, and the per-layer brute-force oracle (Appendix, Section on Profiling Resolution) closes the optimality chain with a maximum gap of 0.018 PPL.\n\n3. **Honest framing of the negative result.** The paper clearly states that only 1/9 models benefits (N=1 spread), qualifies all diagnostic thresholds as \"preliminary,\" and acknowledges the KIVI composability limitation (KIVI+MCKP = KIVI alone on Qwen) in Section 5.1. This is unusually frank for the genre.\n\n4. **Multi-seed stability.** The 3-seed calibration stability analysis (mean PPL 10.99 +/- 0.06, 90.2% allocation agreement) and cross-dataset stability (>96% agreement on 3 architectures, Table in Appendix Section G) strengthen reproducibility claims.\n\n5. **Cross-architecture compounding curves (Figure 2/Section 5.2).** Testing compounding on all three sensitivity-pattern archetypes (Qwen/Llama/Mistral) with sweeps up to k=256 provides convincing evidence that the cardinality constraint operates in a universally safe regime.\n\n6. **Wide architectural coverage.** Eight models spanning MHA, GQA 2:1 through 8:1, sliding-window, and softcapped attention provide reasonable diversity for an empirical study.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **[Major] Qwen-centric ablation design.** The following experiments are conducted only on Qwen2.5-7B: budget sweep (Table in Appendix E), profiling resolution comparison (Appendix I), multi-seed stability (Appendix I), KV separation (Appendix J), runtime benchmark (Appendix K), per-layer oracle validation (Appendix I). Since Qwen is the only \"spread\" model and the only one that benefits, these ablations cannot confirm generalizability. The cardinality constraint is claimed universal, yet its optimal k is tuned per-model with no principled selection rule beyond \"small sweep on calibration data.\"\n\n2. **[Major] Diagnostic rule validated on training set only.** The 9/9 classification accuracy is computed on the same models used to derive the thresholds (concentration >= 0.50, t1/t5 <= 0.70). With only 1 positive example and 8 negatives, any threshold between the positive (0.61, 0.57) and the nearest negative (Llama: 0.57, 0.85) would achieve 9/9. The claim of \"correctly classifies all nine models\" overstates what is effectively curve-fitting with zero held-out validation. The authors acknowledge this (N=1, \"preliminary\"), but it is still prominently repeated in the abstract, introduction, and conclusion.\n\n3. **[Major] CodeLlama-7B counted in 9/9 without end-to-end evaluation.** Table 14 shows \"---\" for both uniform and constrained PPL on CodeLlama. The diagnostic classification relies on profiling data alone, without actually verifying that constrained MCKP provides no benefit. This weakens the claimed 9/9 accuracy---it is really 8/8 verified + 1 inferred from profiling shape.\n\n4. **[Major] Downstream evaluation lacks confidence intervals for 4/5 models.** Only Qwen has multi-seed error bars in Table (Section 5.3). The remaining models (Yi, Llama, Mistral, Gemma-2) are single-seed, with deltas as small as +/-0.1pp, well within typical evaluation noise. The claim that non-spread models show \"near-neutral\" change is not statistically distinguishable from zero.\n\n5. **[Minor] Inconsistent quantization scheme comparison with KIVI.** The KIVI comparison (Section 5.1) reveals that per-head symmetric quantization produces uniform 4-bit PPL of 15.45 on Qwen vs. KIVI's 9.91. The paper correctly identifies this as a scheme difference, but the practical implication---that the entire paper's contribution is irrelevant when per-channel asymmetric quantization is available---is understated. The stated use case (\"symmetric-only deployment environments---common in hardware accelerators that lack per-channel asymmetric support\") is asserted without citation or evidence of such hardware constraints in 2025+.\n\n6. **[Minor] Budget sweep at 8x is uninformative.** At 8x compression, both methods produce catastrophic PPL (907 vs. 14759). Claiming \"an order of magnitude better\" at PPL=907 has no practical utility---neither is usable. The intermediate 5.3x result (Appendix F) is more informative but only covers Llama/Mistral, not Qwen.\n\n7. **[Minor] Runtime benchmark methodology.** The 8.5% overhead is measured with PyTorch-level per-head quantize/dequantize, which the authors acknowledge is not production-realistic. Without kernel-level benchmarks, the throughput claim provides limited guidance for practitioners.\n\n8. **[Minor] Long-context evaluation limited to 8K.** The dramatic result (Qwen uniform 11.15x at 8K) is compelling but only covers 2 models and stops at 8K tokens, far below production context lengths (32K-128K). The claim of \"amplification with longer sequences\" is an extrapolation.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. For the cardinality constraint k selection: is there a principled stopping criterion beyond the minimum of the U-shaped curve? Could you characterize the relationship between k and model properties (head count, GQA ratio) to enable k selection without a compounding sweep?\n\n2. The KIVI+MCKP composability experiment shows no additive benefit. Have you tested whether KIVI with per-head mixed-precision (e.g., some heads at 4-bit KIVI, others at 2-bit KIVI) could recover the benefit?\n\n3. For the compounding sweep (Figure 2), are the k heads selected by the same sensitivity ranking? If so, does the compounding onset depend on which heads are selected (top-k most sensitive vs. top-k least sensitive)?\n\n4. The super-additivity coefficients (1.56x at k=8, 4.89x at k=64) are measured on Qwen. What are the corresponding values for Llama and Mistral?\n\n5. Why does the constrained MCKP produce PPL slightly worse than uniform on Gemma-2 (+0.03) and Phi-3-medium (+0.01)? If the solver knows uniform is feasible, it should not produce worse results unless the cardinality constraint forces unnecessary 2-bit assignments.\n\n### Missing Experiments / Evidence Gaps\n\n1. **Multi-seed downstream evaluation for non-Qwen models.** The current single-seed results cannot support claims about near-zero deltas being meaningful.\n\n2. **CodeLlama end-to-end constrained MCKP evaluation.** Required to honestly claim 9/9.\n\n3. **Held-out validation of the diagnostic rule.** At minimum, one additional model not used for threshold derivation should be tested. The Qwen-14B and Pythia additions are not truly independent since Qwen-14B is the same family and Pythia's flat pattern is trivially classified.\n\n4. **Compounding sweep on non-Qwen models.** The cross-architecture compounding (Figure 2) uses all-heads-sorted-by-sensitivity sweeps, but the U-shaped budget optimization curve (Figure 1) showing the optimal k is only demonstrated on Qwen. How do we know k=2 is optimal for Llama/Mistral without their own U-shaped curves?\n\n5. **Oracle comparison on non-Qwen models.** The per-layer brute-force oracle validates the additive distortion assumption, but only on Qwen. If the assumption holds better on flat/spike models (as expected), showing this explicitly would strengthen the argument.\n\n6. **Runtime benchmark on a model where MCKP helps.** Currently measured only on Qwen, but the \"is it worth the overhead?\" question needs the answer to be on the same model where there is a PPL benefit.\n\n### Claim-Evidence Alignment Issues\n\n1. **\"Solver choice irrelevant\" claim.** The claim is empirically verified on 3 architectures under constrained allocation. However, the theoretical argument (Section 3.4) requires \"no ties in the top-k marginal benefit ranking.\" This condition is not verified for all 9 models---only the 3 explicitly checked. On Mistral, 1/256 differs, attributed to a \"zero-distortion tie with no PPL impact,\" but this edge case slightly undercuts the universality framing.\n\n2. **\"9/9 correct diagnostic.\"** As noted, one model (CodeLlama) lacks end-to-end evaluation, and the rule is fit on all data with no holdout. A more accurate claim would be \"8/8 verified, consistent with 9/9 under profiling-based inference.\"\n\n3. **\"29.4% reduction\" headline number.** The paper uses the high-resolution profiling result (10.91) in the main tables and abstract, which is the best-case Qwen number. The standard-resolution result (11.23, still a 27.3% reduction) and the multi-seed mean (10.99) are buried in appendices. The headline number is technically correct (a single seed under hires profiling) but not the most representative.\n\n4. **Cross-dataset stability \"96% agreement.\"** On Llama, 253/256 heads are forced to 4-bit by the k=2 constraint. The paper acknowledges this inflates agreement (Appendix G), but the headline \">96% on all three architectures\" benefits from this inflation. On Qwen (where all heads are variable), the 96.4% agreement is genuinely informative.\n\n5. **\"Non-uniform allocation provides no meaningful benefit\" for 8/9 models.** On Llama, constrained MCKP achieves 8.45 vs. uniform 8.47 (a 0.02 improvement). While this is negligible practically, the direction is positive. The \"no benefit\" framing is defensible but borders on overstating---more precisely, the benefit is within noise without multi-seed validation.\n\n### Overall Assessment\nScore: 4/6\nConfidence: 4/5\n\nThe paper makes a useful empirical contribution by identifying and fixing 2-bit compounding in per-head KV cache quantization. The methodology is sound (MCKP + cardinality constraint is a clean formulation), the compounding characterization is thorough, and the honest framing that only 1/9 models benefits is refreshing. However, the experimental design is too Qwen-centric for a paper claiming cross-architecture generality: most ablations run on only one model, the diagnostic is fit without holdout, CodeLlama's classification is unverified end-to-end, and downstream results lack error bars on 4/5 models. The practical impact is further limited by the KIVI composability result showing the contribution is subsumed by existing per-channel methods. A solid empirical analysis paper that needs broader ablation coverage and statistical rigor to reach the acceptance bar."}],"totalDurationMs":183843,"totalTokens":69074,"totalToolUseCount":28,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":866,"cache_read_input_tokens":65219,"output_tokens":2988,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":866},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2988,"cache_read_input_tokens":65219,"cache_creation_input_tokens":866,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":866,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":15,"searchCount":13,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01DzBM7cXpMiXJ6DoN9XcBEm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have returned their independent assessments. Now I need to run the full roundtable:\n- Step 1: Independent reviews (done - R1: 4/6, R2: 3/6)\n- Step 2: Cross-sharing (share each reviewer's points with the other)\n- Step 3: Convergence (identify agreements, disagreements, final scores)\n- Step 4: Synthesize final report\n\nLet me now do Step 2 - cross-sharing, where I share each reviewer's key points with the other and let them react. Then Step 3 convergence and Step 4 synthesis.\n\n\nI'll send both reviewers back in with the other's feedback so they can see where they align and where they diverge.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2901,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7461,"cache_read_input_tokens":64654,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7461},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f6b0b3c4-17a1-4fb2-92fd-0f4f71a4e809","uuid":"6c1fea7e-96fa-43d1-8089-f0409d34994b"}
